This guide explains how to set up your computer so that you can

run the notebooks of the book and change them;

build the whole book as a website, with every output and figure already computed;

publish that website on GitHub Pages, without any computation on GitHub.

You only need steps 1 and 2 to study the book. Step 3 is for those who want to publish their own copy (for example, the author, or a student who forks the repository).

After working through this chapter you will be able to:

explain what it means for a program to learn from data, and tell the main kinds of learning problems apart (regression, classification, clustering, reinforcement learning);

represent a dataset as a matrix \mathbf{X} and a vector \mathbf{y}, and read their shapes;

formulate learning as the minimization of a loss, and compute a baseline to compare against;

derive the normal equations of least squares, interpret them geometrically, and verify them in code;

explain overfitting and underfitting, and why a model must be evaluated on data it has not seen;

run a complete workflow split → preprocess → fit → evaluate with scikit-learn;

set up the environment, run the notebooks, and build and publish this book as a static website (see the 

Setup Guide).

Prerequisites. Vectors, matrices, dot products, the gradient. Nothing else.

How to read this chapter. The first sections build intuition; Sections 1.6–1.8 are the technical core; the exercises at the end are the best way to check your understanding. Every code cell can be executed, and changed, in your own copy of the notebook.

Chapter 1 showed the whole story of machine learning in miniature. Before we study methods one by one, we make sure that the tools underneath them are solid. A large part of the bugs in machine learning code are not “machine learning” bugs at all: they are a wrong array shape, a silent broadcast, a feature on the wrong scale, or a test sample that leaked into the preprocessing. This chapter is about avoiding those.

After working through this chapter you will be able to:

create, index, and reshape NumPy arrays, and explain the difference between a view and a copy;

write vectorized code and use broadcasting correctly, checking shapes before trusting results;

use numpy.linalg for the operations that machine learning needs (solving systems, norms, eigendecompositions), and recognize the main numerical traps;

load, inspect, filter, group, and summarize tabular data with pandas;

produce clear, honest figures with the object-oriented interface of Matplotlib;

use the language of probability: conditional probability and Bayes’ rule, random variables, expectation, variance, covariance, the Gaussian distribution (univariate and multivariate), the law of large numbers and the central limit theorem;

derive the maximum likelihood estimates of a Bernoulli and a Gaussian model, and explain why least squares is maximum likelihood under Gaussian noise;

preprocess data: scale features, transform skewed ones, impute missing values, encode categories, and assemble everything in a leak-free scikit-learn pipeline.

Prerequisites. Chapter 1. Basic programming (variables, loops, functions); the next section is a five-minute refresher in case it has been a while. Calculus of one variable and the matrix notation of Chapter 1.

How to read this chapter. It is a toolbox, not a story, so you can read it in pieces: Sections 2.3 to 2.5 are about software, Section 2.6 about probability, and Section 2.7 ties everything together with a real preprocessing pipeline. If you already know NumPy and pandas, skim Sections 2.3 and 2.4, but do not skip the Pitfall boxes: they describe mistakes that experienced people still make.

In Chapter 1 we trained models and measured them on data they had not seen. In Chapter 2 we learned that an average over a sample is an estimate of an expectation. This chapter puts the two ideas together. It answers a question that every method in the rest of the book silently relies on: why should a function that has a small error on the training set have a small error on future data? The answer is not “always”: we will see exactly when it holds, how fast it improves with the number of examples, and what it depends on.

After working through this chapter you will be able to:

define the data distribution, the expected risk and the empirical risk, and explain why the whole of machine learning is about the gap between them;

derive the Bayes-optimal predictor for the squared loss and for the 0–1 loss, and explain why a part of the error can never be removed;

write empirical risk minimization (ERM) and split the excess risk into an approximation error and an estimation error;

explain, with a short proof and with experiments, why the training error is optimistic, and quantify the optimism when many candidate models are compared;

state Markov’s, Chebyshev’s and Hoeffding’s inequalities, and derive Hoeffding’s inequality;

prove a generalization bound for a finite hypothesis class with the union bound, and turn it into a sample-complexity formula;

define shattering, the growth function and the VC dimension, compute them for simple classes, and state the VC generalization bound;

define the Rademacher complexity, derive its bound for norm-bounded linear classifiers, and explain why controlling the norm (regularization, large margins) controls generalization even when the dimension is larger than the number of examples;

state the No Free Lunch theorem and explain what an inductive bias is;

use Hoeffding’s inequality to size a test set, and judge honestly how loose the bounds of the theory are on a real data set.

Prerequisites. Chapters 1 and 2, in particular Sections 1.5 (loss and risk) and 2.6 (expectation, variance, the law of large numbers). Calculus and the matrix notation of the previous chapters. No measure theory: random variables are treated as in Chapter 2.

How to read this chapter. It is the most mathematical chapter of Part I, and it is built so that you can read it at two depths. Each section has the same rhythm: an idea in words, a precise statement, a proof (marked, and safe to skip at a first reading), and an experiment that makes the statement concrete. The experiments carry the message; the proofs explain why the message is true. If you skip every proof you will still be able to follow the book. If you read them all, you will have seen the three classical ways of measuring the capacity of a model: its size (Section 3.6), its shattering power (Section 3.7) and its ability to fit noise (Section 3.8).

A word about the data in this chapter

Theory talks about a distribution \mathcal{D} that is unknown in practice. To check a statement about \mathcal{D} numerically we need a world where we do know it. In Sections 3.2, 3.3 and 3.4 we therefore use a small simulated world, and from Section 3.6 on we also use a real data set (Breast Cancer, seen in Chapter 1), treated as the population from which we draw samples. Whenever a number comes from a simulation, the text says so.

Section 3.4 showed that Hoeffding’s guarantee for one fixed function cannot be applied to \hat f_S, because \hat f_S was chosen with the help of the data. The remedy is to ask for more: a bound that holds simultaneously for all functions of \mathcal{H}, hence also for the one that the data selected. For a finite class the union bound does it: for any events A_1, \dots, A_m,\mathbb{P}\Big(\bigcup_{j=1}^m A_j\Big) \le \sum_{j=1}^m \mathbb{P}(A_j).

Let \mathcal{H} be a finite set with \lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert functions and let the loss take values in [0, 1].

Theorem 3.1 (uniform deviation for a finite class). For every \delta \in (0, 1), with probability at least 1 - \delta over the draw of the training set S of n i.i.d. examples, all functions f \in \mathcal{H} satisfy\boxed{\ \lvert R(f) - \hat{R}_S(f)\rvert \le \sqrt{\frac{\ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert + \ln(2/\delta)}{2n}}\ }

Proof. Let \varepsilon denote the right-hand side. For each fixed f, Hoeffding’s inequality gives \mathbb{P}(\lvert R(f) - \hat{R}_S(f)\rvert > \varepsilon) \le 2e^{-2n\varepsilon^2} = \delta/\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert, because 2n\varepsilon^2 = \ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert + \ln(2/\delta). By the union bound over the \lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert functions, the probability that at least one of them deviates by more than \varepsilon is at most \lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert \cdot \delta / \lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert = \delta. \square

The price of uniformity is the term \ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert: the size of the class enters only through its logarithm. A class with a million functions costs \ln 10^6 \approx 13.8; a class with 

10100 functions costs 230. This is the mathematical form of the optimism of Section 3.4.1, and it explains why the curves of Figure 3.4 grew like \sqrt{\ln m}.

Corollary 3.2 (what ERM guarantees). On the event of Theorem 3.1, with \varepsilon its right-hand side, the ERM solution satisfiesR(\hat f_S) \le R(f_{\mathcal{H}}) + 2\varepsilon, \qquad\text{that is,}\qquad \text{estimation error} \le 2\sqrt{\frac{\ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert + \ln(2/\delta)}{2n}}.

Proof. Three inequalities, in a chain: R(\hat f_S) \le \hat{R}_S(\hat f_S) + \varepsilon (uniform deviation, applied to \hat f_S), then \hat{R}_S(\hat f_S) \le \hat{R}_S(f_{\mathcal{H}}) (definition of ERM), then \hat{R}_S(f_{\mathcal{H}}) \le R(f_{\mathcal{H}}) + \varepsilon (uniform deviation, applied to f_{\mathcal{H}}). \square

So the estimation error of ERM over a finite class shrinks like \sqrt{\ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert / n}: the more candidates, the more data are needed, but only logarithmically in the number of candidates. Inverting the formulas gives the sample complexity: to have all deviations at most \varepsilon, or an estimation error at most \varepsilon (which needs deviations of \varepsilon/2), it suffices thatn \ge \frac{\ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert + \ln(2/\delta)}{2\varepsilon^2}
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
n \ge \frac{2\,\big(\ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert + \ln(2/\delta)\big)}{\varepsilon^2}
\quad\text{respectively.}

Chapter 3 showed that learning is possible when the class of functions is not too rich, and that the price of richness is a gap between training and test error. This chapter makes that statement concrete with the simplest learning method there is. The k-nearest-neighbor (k-NN) method has no training phase and no parameters to fit: to predict at a new point it looks at the k closest training points and averages their labels. Despite its simplicity it is a serious method (it is still used, and it is hard to beat on some problems), it is completely analyzable, and its single hyperparameter k moves smoothly from a very flexible model (k = 1) to a very rigid one (k = n). That makes it the ideal laboratory for the bias–variance trade-off, the idea that organizes the whole of Part II.

After working through this chapter you will be able to:

write the k-NN classifier and k-NN regressor, implement them from scratch with the vectorized distances of Chapter 2, and check your code against scikit-learn;

explain how k controls the flexibility of the decision boundary and of the fitted curve, and why the training error can never be used to choose it;

derive the bias–variance decomposition of the squared error, and use it to compute exactly the bias and the variance of k-NN regression;

explain, and verify by experiment, why the best k grows with the sample size while the neighborhoods shrink;

state the Cover–Hart theorem (the 1-NN error is at most twice the Bayes error) and the consistency of k-NN when k \to \infty and k/n \to 0;

describe the curse of dimensionality in three ways (empty neighborhoods, concentration of distances, slow rates), measure it on real data, and say what helps;

choose a distance and a weighting scheme sensibly, and explain when tree-based neighbor search (KD-trees, ball trees) is faster than brute force and when it is not.

Prerequisites. Chapters 1 to 3. In particular the vectorized distances of Worked example 2.1 and Exercise 2.7, the standardization of Section 2.7, and the Bayes predictor and the approximation–estimation split of Sections 3.2 and 3.3.

A word about the data in this chapter

The experiments use real data sets that ship with scikit-learn: Digits (images of handwritten digits), Wine, Diabetes and Breast Cancer. To check the bias–variance formulas exactly we need a world in which the true function is known, so Sections 4.5 and 4.6 and part of Section 4.7 use simulated data. Whenever that is the case, the text says so.

